  </html:div>
<html:p>Read down the third column and the datacenter line tells one story: each generation moves the matrix multiply further away from the individual thread. <html:code>mma.sync</html:code> is issued by a <fr:link href="/X9VZ/" title="Warp" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/X9VZ/" display-uri="X9VZ" type="local">warp</fr:link> and the operands live in its registers; <html:code>wgmma</html:code> is issued by a warpgroup of four and can read the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> operand straight out of <fr:link href="/8GZN/" title="Shared memory (GPU)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/8GZN/" display-uri="8GZN" type="local">shared memory</fr:link>; <html:code>tcgen05.mma</html:code> is issued by a single thread on behalf of a <fr:link href="/W2D5/" title="Cooperative Thread Array (CTA)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/W2D5/" display-uri="W2D5" type="local">CTA</fr:link> pair and keeps its accumulator in <fr:link href="/YCAO/" title="Tensor Memory (TMEM)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/YCAO/" display-uri="YCAO" type="local">Tensor Memory (TMEM)</fr:link>, a memory the register file does not see. The data movement follows the same arc — <html:code>cp.async</html:code> is a per-thread copy, <fr:link href="/JHA9/" title="Tensor Memory Accelerator (TMA)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/JHA9/" display-uri="JHA9" type="local">Tensor Memory Accelerator (TMA)</fr:link> is a descriptor a single thread hands to a hardware engine.</html:p><html:p>The trap in the table is <html:code>sm_120a</html:code>. Consumer Blackwell shares the marketing generation with B200 but not the tensor-core path: it has Hopper-era conveniences (TMA, clusters, <fr:link href="/OGCS8/" title="Distributed Shared Memory (GPU)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/OGCS8/" display-uri="OGCS8" type="local">Distributed <fr:link href="/8GZN/" title="Shared memory (GPU)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/8GZN/" display-uri="8GZN" type="local">Shared Memory (GPU)</fr:link></fr:link>) and Ampere-era MMA, with no <html:code>tcgen05</html:code> and no TMEM at all. A kernel written against <html:code>sm_100a</html:code> does not merely run slower on a 5090 — it does not compile. Treat <html:code>sm_120a</html:code> as the branch point in a kernel's dispatch, not as a small delta from <html:code>sm_100a</html:code>.</html:p>
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(219, 216, 215);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(109, 103, 101);">Remark.</html:span> The trailing <html:code>a</html:code> means <html:em>architecture-specific</html:em>: the target enables instructions exclusive to that exact chip and gives up forward compatibility, so the resulting cubin will not PTX-JIT onto a later architecture the way a plain <html:code>sm_90</html:code> binary would. Every generation from Hopper on ships its tensor-core ISA only behind an <html:code>a</html:code> target, which is why a real build matrix has one entry per row above rather than one per generation. <html:code>sm_121a</html:code> is the extreme case — the same instruction surface as <html:code>sm_120a</html:code> and still its own cubin.</html:div>
</fr:mainmatter>
